preConditionHook:
// ScratchExitOnEarlyOppositeStrongBar -- Grimes A list (G06-20, key
// ScratchExitOnEarlyOppositeStrongBar). Authored 2026-09-22 as a new
// scratchWindow input on ExitOnOppositeStrongTrendBar and split out as its own
// rule 2026-11-07, because the 202611 BRK and OWN templates place
// ExitOnOppositeStrongTrendBar, so that rule stays exactly as those families
// run it. Code otherwise unchanged.
//
// "Developing price action may suggest the trend is losing integrity --
// scratch the trade." ExitOnOppositeStrongTrendBar's test, restricted to the
// fill bar and the scratchWindow bars after it, turns a trail into a SCRATCH:
// the trade comes off for a small win or loss instead of being held to the
// original stop. ExitOnWeakFollowThrough carries exactly this kind of
// BarsSinceEntry cap as maxBarsAfterSpike. The default scratchWindow(3) is the
// merged candidate's value, Grimes's "first few bars".
//
// THE TEST: one strong trend bar against the position is enough to leave, even
// though flipping direction would take two -- the mechanizable half of the
// source's exit/reversal asymmetry. The long side exits on a strong BEAR bar: a
// body worth at least bodyFraction of the bar's range and no more than
// tailFraction of it left as a tail on the far side (close near the low).
// Body and tail as PRODUCTS, not quotients: EL raises a runtime
// divide-by-zero and a bar whose high equals its low is legal data.
//
// scratchWindow 0 MEANS OFF, not "the fill bar only": at 0 the gate is open
// at any age and this is ExitOnOppositeStrongTrendBar exactly. The grid
// therefore starts at 1 and the fill-bar-only case is not reachable.
// ctx.BarsSinceEntry() is VERIFIED as 0 on the fill bar, 0 while flat, and
// restarting through a reversal; the MarketPosition guard is what keeps the
// flat-bar 0 from mattering. EL does not short-circuit `or`, but neither half of
// the gate has a side effect, so the C++ `||` is equivalent.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 0.
